I have analyzed the Delta WebSocket handler and the ticker state update logic, and implemented a force-update mechanism that guarantees WebSocket price packets take strict priority over polled REST API data.
Root Cause Analysis
REST API Polling Overwrite: In App.tsx, fetchMarketData() polled /api/delta/markets every 3 seconds. When the REST response resolved, it unconditionally performed a shallow merge over next[item.symbol], clobbering higher-frequency live WebSocket markPrice packets with older REST snapshot prices.
Packet Type & Field Fragmentation: In useDeltaWebSocket.ts, incoming price packets could provide price values under different keys (mark_price, markPrice, close, price, last_price) or slightly varying channel wrappers.
What Was Implemented
Immediate Force-Update on Incoming WebSocket Packets (src/hooks/useDeltaWebSocket.ts & src/App.tsx):
Expanded ticker packet identification to detect any ticker message (v2/ticker, ticker, or payloads containing mark_price / markPrice).
Normalizes and extracts markPrice and indexPrice with exact packet timestamps.
In App.tsx:handleTickerFromWs, incoming WebSocket packets immediately and unconditionally override the existing state for the symbol and its alias (e.g., BTCUSD and BTCUSDT).
Records the authoritative WebSocket timestamp in lastWsPriceTimestampsRef.
Concurrently updates latestMarketsRef.current and recalculates any active open positions' markPrice and unrealisedPnl in real time.
WebSocket Prioritization Guard against REST Polls (src/App.tsx):
In fetchMarketData(), before applying polled REST data for any symbol, the code checks lastWsPriceTimestampsRef.current[sym].
If live WebSocket updates have been received for that symbol, the polled REST data is forbidden from overwriting markPrice.
The authoritative WebSocket markPrice is preserved, merging only non-price secondary metadata (e.g., open interest, volume totals) while ensuring the price display is never overwritten by stale REST responses.
